; 04-split-into-cases.kurt
;
; Split into cases
;
; For a proof from `A or B`, prove the same conclusion in an `A` case and a `B` case. The
; propositional theory's `or-elim` rule combines the disjunction and the two implications produced
; by closing the case blocks.

load prop
bool A, B, C, E

use A or B
use A implies C
use B implies C

show C
proof
    case A
        C
    case B
        C
    C
qed

; A `case` block behaves like `assume` when it closes: the first block produces `A implies C`, and
; the second produces `B implies C`. The final `C` is the explicit `or-elim` step.
;
; Three or four alternatives work the same way, with a `case` for each ("or-elim-3", "or-elim-4"
; in prop.kurt):

bool G
use A or B or E
use A implies G
use B implies G
use E implies G
case A
    G
case B
    G
case E
    G
G

; For more alternatives, combine the cases in stages: a `case B or E or ...` block around the cases
; of the rest, which closes with its own conclusion.
